ana: Heads-up — updating the Next starter to remove SWR and use a tiny fetch hook for embeddings. I hit a funky auth race when two pages request embeddings at the same time and the access token expires.

ryan: Oof. Did the 401 come back mid-flight or on refresh?

ana: Mid-flight. One request triggered refresh, others lost the race and failed with 401. Our hook didn't replay.

jasmin: We had this with SSR preview routes too. Pattern we use: a shared refreshPromise so inflight requests await the refresh instead of each triggering it.

ana: Implemented in the starter:

```ts
let refreshPromise: Promise<void> | null = null;
async function withAuthRetry(fn) {
  try { return await fn(); }
  catch (e) {
    if (e.message === 'unauthorized') {
      if (!refreshPromise) refreshPromise = auth.refresh();
      await refreshPromise;
      refreshPromise = null;
      return await fn();
    }
    throw e;
  }
}
```

ryan: Nice. Add a small jest test that simulates two concurrent calls and ensures the second succeeds after the first triggers refresh.

ana: Adding test. Also noticed server-side forwarding (api/embeddings -> hosted SDK) behaves slightly differently than client fetch (CORS and token layering). Should we document server vs client auth tradeoffs?

jasmin: Yes — add a note in README: prefer server-side embedding calls when possible; for client usage include withAuthRetry. Link to Console token lifetime.

